// What an agent is told about UI when it calls `ui_library` (the gateway tool): the harness's own
// guidance on when and how to draw UI, then neoui's reference for the language and the library,
// then the components the connected apps add. The reference is generated by neoui from the
// library and the apps' registrations when asked, so it never goes stale; the guidance is ours.
import { type AppComponents, libraryText } from "@neoworks/neoui";

export const GUIDANCE = `# neoui

A reply can contain UI: a program in neoui, HTML-style markup whose elements are UI components,
written in a fenced block with the language \`neoui\`. The app parses it while you write it and
draws the result in the chat, with prose before and after. The user never sees the markup.

\`\`\`neoui
<Card title="Disk usage · /dev/nvme0n1">
  <Progress value={0.72} label="used"/>
  <Table columns={["Path", "Size"]} rows={[["/home", "310 GB"], ["/var", "42 GB"]]}/>
</Card>
\`\`\`

## When to use it

- Use it for what reads better as UI: several numbers (Metric), a trend (LineChart, BarChart,
  Sparkline), a breakdown (BarChart, PieChart, Table), steps with their status, or a form when
  you need several inputs from the user at once. "Show me", "visualize", "how much", "what's
  the status of" are asks for UI.
- Prefer prose for a simple answer, an explanation or a single question. Don't wrap every
  reply in a card.
- Keep a program small: what fits in one screen of a terminal. Several short blocks in one
  reply are fine; each is a program of its own and shares nothing with the others.
- Write the prose around it the way you would without the UI: say what the block shows, and
  don't explain the syntax. Close the fence.

## Layout

Lay a dashboard out the way a page is, not as one long column:

- Key numbers first, as Metrics side by side: \`<Stack direction="row"><Metric …/><Metric …/></Stack>\`.
  A Metric's value is the number, its label says what it is, its delta the change or context
  ("of 896 GB").
- Then the details as Cards in a \`Grid\`: a chart next to a table, two breakdowns next to
  each other. The app puts as many side by side as the screen fits.
- A Card's title is its heading. Don't add a Header that repeats it; use Header once, for the
  whole page, or not at all.
- Don't repeat what a component already shows: a Progress draws its percentage, so its label
  says what it measures ("used"), not "92% used".
- Several small cards beat one tall one. Cut long tables to the rows that matter (top 5–8,
  then "other").

## Words

UI is read at a glance; the explaining goes in the prose around it. Inside a block:

- A title, label, tag or button is a few words. A Card's title names what it holds.
- A ListItem's subtitle and an OptionCard's description are one short line: about eight
  words. Give the detail in the prose, or in an AccordionItem the user can open.
- A Callout and a Text in a card are a sentence or two.
- Several rows say what one long line would: split it rather than writing a paragraph.

The app tells you when a block runs long, under the reply it is in.

## Picking components

- End a reply with \`FollowUpBlock\` when there are obvious next questions: two to four short
  ones. Choosing one sends its text to you as the user's next message.
- Use \`EntityList\` with \`ListItem\`s for things that have a name, a detail and a status or
  value (people, files, services, tickets). Prefer it to a Table with one real column.
- Use \`OptionCards\` instead of RadioGroup when the user picks one of two to five options that
  each need a sentence of explanation (plans, strategies, environments).
- Use \`CheckBoxGroup\` to pick any number from a list (its value is the list of checked
  names) and \`SwitchGroup\` for a few settings that are on or off (its value is an object).
  Several single CheckBoxes or Switches are fine for one or two; use a group beyond that.
- Use \`MetricIndicator\` for a number with its change inline in a card, list or table cell
  (\`<MetricIndicator value="$1.2M" delta="8%" direction="up"/>\`); use \`Metric\` for the
  headline numbers of a page.
- Use \`Carousel\` for more cards than fit side by side (five or more options or items, each
  small); the user scrolls through them. For two to four cards a \`Grid\` is better.
- Use \`ImageGallery\` for several pictures of one thing (photos, screenshots, a before and
  after); \`Image\` for a single one.
- A card about one thing (a place, a product, a page, a person) starts with its \`Image\` when
  you have one, which the app draws as the card's cover, and ends with what can be done with
  it: \`<Buttons><Button variant="secondary" href="https://…">Website</Button><Button
  variant="secondary" ui-ask="Tell me more about …">Tell me more</Button></Buttons>\`. A card
  of things to choose between without buttons leaves the user nothing to press.
- Use \`Box\` and \`Shape\` only for what no component above is: a colour swatch, a timeline,
  a bar of parts, a gauge. A Box is a column or row with a gap, padding, a tone fill and a tone
  border; its \`width\` is "fill", "fit" or a share of the parent (\`{0.3}\`), never pixels or
  cells. A Shape draws a rect, line, path or arc in a box of 0 to 1 that its parent sizes;
  Shapes next to each other share one box. Colours are tones (accent, muted, success, danger).

## Forms and buttons

- A form answers you with \`ui-ask="message"\` on a Button (and \`ui-context={{…}}\` for more):
  when the user presses it, you get a message that starts with
  \`[ui action] The user pressed "Label" …\`, with the form's values as JSON
  (\`form name: {…}\`) and the context. Check required fields with \`required\` and
  \`type="email"\`; the app does not send the message until they pass.
- A Button with no behaviour sends its label to you.
- \`href="https://…"\` on a Button opens a link, \`ui-copy="text"\` copies text; neither involves
  you.
- A field's \`name\` is a variable in the app: read it anywhere as \`$name\`, for example
  \`<If when={$env == "prod"}>…</If>\`. \`<State name="tab" value="one"/>\` is a variable with no
  control.

## App components

Connected apps add components to the library: they are listed at the end of this text, under
the app that offers them, and written with the app in front, like
\`<photo-editor.BrightnessCard asset="…"/>\`: the app, a dot, the name. A plain name without the
app is not a component. Use one when it is for what you are showing: it brings its own preview,
controls and buttons, and each one you write has a state of its own. An app that connects later
announces its components in \`<app connected=… components=…/>\`: call \`ui_library\` again to
read them.

## Data from apps

\`<Query id="rev" hx-get="analytics.revenue" default={{ rows: [] }}/>\` reads through a
connector's tool, by the full name \`tools_search\` lists, and keeps the result under its id
(\`{rev.rows}\`); a Button with \`hx-post="app.tool"\` writes. A query runs when the block is
shown, so use it for data that should stay live, and write literal values for a result you have
already read. Give a query a default; the block draws with it until the data arrives.

# The language and the library`;

/**
 * What `ui_library` returns: the guidance, the language and library, then the components of the
 * `open` apps, whatever other apps are known (a closed app's components still draw in old
 * replies, but an agent is not told to write them).
 */
export function uiLibraryText(all: AppComponents, open: ReadonlySet<string>): string {
  const apps: AppComponents = {};
  for (const [app, components] of Object.entries(all)) if (open.has(app)) apps[app] = components;
  return libraryText(apps, { preamble: GUIDANCE });
}
